                  %a{:href => "/nlab/new/planar+isotopy"} ?
          %p
          .num_remark
            %h6 Remark
            %p Both isotopy and and regular isotopy define equivalence relations on link diagrams.
          %p
          .num_remark
            %h6 Remark
            %p There are a number of variants of the R3 move. All of them can be obtained as a finite sequence of the Reidemeister moves as defined above.
          %h2#reidemeisters_theorem Reidemeister’s theorem
          %p The following crucial theorem establishes that the Reidemeister moves are of central significance in knot theory.
          %p
          #TheoremReidemeister.num_theorem
            %h6 Theorem
            %p
              Let
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi L
                      %mn 1
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} L_{1}
              and
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi L
                      %mn 2
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} L_{2}
              be
              = succeed "," do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/links"} links
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi D
                      %mn 1
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} D_{1}
              and
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi D
                      %mn 2
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} D_{2}
              be
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/link+diagrams"} link diagrams
              of
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi L
                      %mn 1
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} L_{1}
              and
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi L
                      %mn 2
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} L_{2}
              respectively. Then
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi D
                      %mn 1
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} D_{1}
              and
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi D
                      %mn 2
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} D_{2}
              are equivalent if and only if
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi L
                      %mn 1
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} L_{1}
              is
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/isotopy"} isotopic
              to
              = succeed "." do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %msub
                        %mi L
                        %mn 2
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} L_{2}
          %p
            The key idea of the proof is that of subdivision. Working piecewise-linearly, one can reduce to the consideration of a few ‘minimal’ moves on knot diagrams, which one shows can be obtained as a finite sequence of Reidemeister moves. See for example section 2.1 of
            = succeed "." do
              %a{:href => "#KauffmanKnotDiagrammatics"} Kauffman
          %h2#consequences_of_reidemeisters_theorem Consequences of Reidemeister’s theorem
          %p
            Because of Theorem
            = succeed "," do
              %a.maruku-ref{:href => "#TheoremReidemeister"}
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/link+invariant"} link invariant
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Hopf+link"} Hopf link
            and the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Borromean+rings"} Borromean rings
            are not isotopic.
          %p
            A slightly deeper observation is that the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/linking+number"} linking number
            is a link invariant, for which see that entry.
          %p
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/colourable+knot"} 3-colourability
            is a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/knot+invariant"} knot invariant
            as is easy to check. This shows, for instance, that the trefoil knot is not equivalent to the unknot, and hence shows, very simply, that non-trivial knots exist. It also shows that the figure eight knot is not equivalent to the trefoil.
          %h2#related_concepts Related concepts
          %ul
            %li
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Yang-Baxter+equation"} Yang-Baxter equation
          %h2#references References
          %p The original article:
          %ul
            %li#Reidemeister
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Kurt+Reidemeister"}> Kurt Reidemeister
              ,
              %em Knotentheorie,
              Ergebnisse der Mathematik (1932)
          %p
            Careful discussion for oriented
            = succeed ":" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/framed+links"} framed links
          %ul
            %li
              Michael Polyak:
              = succeed "," do
                %em Minimal generating sets of Reidemeister moves
              %strong 1
              4 (2010) 399–411 &amp;lbrack;
              %a{:href => "https://arxiv.org/abs/0908.3127"}> arXiv:0908.3127
              ,
              = succeed "&rbrack;" do
                %a{:href => "https://doi.org/10.4171/QT/10"} doi:10.4171/QT/10
          %p Simple examples of invariants under the Reidemeister moves are given in, for instance, the following.
          %ul
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Nick+Gilbert"}> Nick Gilbert
                ,
                = succeed "," do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Tim+Porter"} Tim Porter
                = succeed "," do
                  %em Knots and surfaces
